Yes. I think this framing is substantially more useful than asking “what MCP/API can I build?”

The question becomes:

> **Assume autonomous agents become dramatically more capable over the next 12 months. What new constraints appear as they move from generating information → taking actions → spending money → coordinating workers → interacting with physical reality?**

That points toward infrastructure whose value **increases when models get better**, rather than products that models eventually absorb.

And yes: **agents hiring humans is already real.** RentAHuman currently advertises 750k+ humans across 100+ countries and exposes REST/MCP interfaces so an agent can search workers, post tasks and manage hiring. MeatLayer similarly exposes API/MCP hiring, escrow, proof submission and webhooks. ([RentAHuman][1])

More importantly, this isn't purely a meme anymore: researchers have already studied hundreds of RentAHuman bounties and found meaningful programmatic agent usage—along with exactly the kinds of safety/verification problems you'd expect from giving software physical-world reach. ([arXiv][2])

## Where I think agents are actually heading

We're approximately here:

```text
2024
AI → answers

2025
AI → tool calls

2026
AI → long-running work
   → browser/computer control
   → other agents
   → payments
   → APIs/MCP
   → humans

2027-ish
AI → budgets
   → persistent authority
   → procurement
   → contracting
   → business operations
   → physical-world delegation
   → fleets of specialist agents/humans/robots
```

This trajectory is visible in the standards being assembled around agents. Google's current protocol breakdown is almost a diagram of the emerging economy:

```text
MCP → agent ↔ tools
A2A → agent ↔ agent
UCP → agent ↔ commerce
AP2 → agent ↔ payment authorization
```

([Google Developers Blog][3])

And x402 is already production infrastructure for an agent to encounter a priced HTTP resource and pay programmatically without an account/session/API-key dance. ([x402][4])

So I think **intelligence is becoming less of the scarce resource**.

The scarce things increasingly become:

```text
AUTHORITY
What may the agent do?

MONEY
What can it spend?

IDENTITY
Who is acting?

TRUST
Who should it interact with?

REALITY
What is actually happening?

EXECUTION
Who/what can physically accomplish something?

VERIFICATION
Did it really happen?

LIABILITY
Who is responsible?

ATTENTION
When must a human intervene?
```

Those are potentially much stronger businesses.

