# The key idea: Reality becomes expensive

Consider an extremely capable 2027 agent.

You tell it:

> “Figure out whether these 200 convenience stores actually stock my product.”

The model can reason perfectly.

It can search perfectly.

It can write beautiful code.

It still cannot **look at shelf #4 in a shop in Phnom Penh today**.

It needs:

```text
human
camera
robot
sensor
merchant API
satellite
delivery service
phone call
official record
```

That interface between computation and reality is scarce.

I would call the broad category:

# **Reality infrastructure for agents**

That contains some very interesting API businesses.

---

# 1. Human-as-a-tool API

RentAHuman/MeatLayer prove the category exists. ([RentAHuman][1])

But you don't necessarily want to compete by recruiting 750,000 people.

Instead build a **routing layer across human networks**.

An agent asks:

```http
POST /execute
```

```json
{
  "task": "photograph storefront",
  "location": "Kampot, Cambodia",
  "deadline": "4 hours",
  "budget": 8,
  "proof_required": ["gps", "photo"]
}
```

Your infrastructure finds the appropriate execution network.

Today:

```text
RentAHuman
MeatLayer
traditional gig platform
specialist contractor
local courier
research service
```

Later:

```text
human
robot
drone
autonomous vehicle
local agent
```

The abstraction is not:

> hire a human.

It's:

> **cause X to happen in reality.**

That is much bigger.

