# Where I see the real gold

I'd investigate these five as actual ventures:

**1. RealityRouter**
One API through which an agent can request an outcome, and the system routes it to software, another agent, a human or eventually a robot.

**2. Proof-of-Work / Reality Attestation API**
Neutral verification infrastructure for agent-commissioned physical tasks.

**3. Human Escalation API**
Any autonomous agent can cheaply summon appropriate human cognition/physical intervention when stuck.

**4. Delegated Authority + Budget API**
Machine-readable answer to “is this agent actually authorized to perform/spend/delegate this?”

**5. Work Receipt / Reputation Protocol**
Portable proof that a human/agent/robot performed X, under Y conditions, with Z result.

And **Moltwork becomes considerably more interesting in this framing**. Rather than another “marketplace of AI agents,” it could become a **universal work protocol**:

```text
submit outcome
     ↓
bid / route
     ↓
human | agent | model | service | robot
     ↓
execute
     ↓
prove
     ↓
verify
     ↓
receipt
     ↓
settle
```

That also connects neatly to the earlier version we discussed where model workers claim tasks and produce verifiable execution receipts. The expansion is simply that **workers aren't necessarily models anymore**.

The deepest moat wouldn't be the marketplace UI. It would be the accumulated graph of **what work exists, who/what can perform it, what it actually costs, how reliably they perform it, and what constitutes convincing proof**.

That dataset becomes more valuable every single time autonomous agents become more capable.

